h \\(y=1\\): \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{1}\\).\n* Points with \\(y=2\\): \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{2}\\).\n* The remaining point \\((1,3)\\) has \\(x=1\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_{3}\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq \\ell_{1}\\cup\\ell_{2}\\cup\\ell_{3}.\n\\]\n\nThe three lines are distinct, and none of them is sunny. Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=0\\text{ sunny lines is possible (e.g., }y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x=1\\text{).}}\n\\]"
