1),\\\\\nD&=(1,3), & E&=(3,1), & F&=(2,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=4\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAmong all lines determined by the six points, the only ones that are non\u2011sunny **and contain three points** are:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{h}: y=1\\)\u2003through \\(A(1,1),\\,C(2,1),\\,E(3,1)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{v}: x=1\\)\u2003through \\(A(1,1),\\,B(1,2),\\,D(1,3)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{d}: x+y=4\\)\u2003through \\(D(1,3),\\,E(3,1),\\,F(2,2)\\).\n\nEvery other non\u2011sunny line determined by the set contains at most two points (e.g. \\(y=2\\) contains \\(B,F\\), \\(x=2\\) contains \\(C,F\\), \\(x+y=3\\) contains \\(B,C\\), etc.).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Necessity of a 3\u2011point non\u2011sunny line\n\nSuppose we have three distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L_{1},\\mathcal L_{2},\\mathcal L_{3}\\) covering all six points.  \nIf none of the three lines is one of \\(\\ell_{h},\\ell_{v},\\ell_{d}\\), then each line contains at most two of the points, so the total number of incidences is at most \\(3\\cdot2=6\\).  \nSince the six points are distinct, each line would have to contain exactly two points, and consequently **all three lines would be sunny** (a line containing two points cannot be non\u2011sunny unless it is one of the three listed above).  \nThus a configuration with exactly two sunny lines would be impossible in this case.\n\nHence, any configuration with exactly two sunny lines must contain **exactly one** of the three 3\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines, and the other two lines must be sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Examination of the three possibilities\n\nWe treat each possible 3\u2011point non\u2011sunny line separately and show that the remaining three points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(\\ell_{