h line is sunny because its slope belongs to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), none of which is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Coverage of the required points\n\n- \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).\n- \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y = -\\frac12(1)+\\frac52 = 2,\\qquad\n  y = -\\frac12(3)+\\frac52 = 1 .\n  \\]\n- \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y = -2(1)+5 = 3,\\qquad\n  y = -2(2)+5 = 1 .\n  \\]\n\nThus the six points of \\(S_3\\) are all contained in the union\n\\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Distinctness\n\nThe three lines have different slopes, hence they are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\). Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the value  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3}\n\\]\n\nis achievable. This completes the proof that \\(k=3\\) is possible."
