t most two points of \\(S_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of a non‑vertical line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope\n\\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\)).  \nIf two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\) lie on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1)=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(p\\mid (b_2-b_1)\\).  \nConsequently any third lattice point on the same line must be of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_1+ kq,\\;b_1+ kp),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), from (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(a_1+ kq)+(b_1+ kp)=a_1+b_1+k(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)), the inequality can hold for at most two integer values of \\(k\\). Hence at most two points of \\(S_n\\) lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the conditions and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); inside the\ntriangle (3) such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points.  \nTherefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+n(n-k)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the right–hand side equals \\(3\\); for every \\(n\\ge4\\) it is\nstrictly smaller than \\(4\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le 3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) actually occurs.\n\n---\n\n#### \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n