ent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-n = (1-n)(x-1).\n   \\]\n   Its slope \\(1-n\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Hence we obtain a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   *Key observation.* A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it meets each horizontal row \\(R_b\\) in **at most one** point.  \n\n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines.  \n   The horizontal lines can be taken, without loss of generality, to be the rows they cover; thus the rows that are **not** equipped with a horizontal line must be covered solely by sunny lines.  \n\n   Let \\(D\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of row indices without a horizontal line.  \n   Then \\(|D|=k\\) (because we have \\(n\\) rows in total and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines).  \n   Every row \\(b\\in D\\) contains \\(|R_b|=n+1-b\\) points, each of which must be hit by a distinct sunny line (a sunny line can contribute at most one point to that row).  \n   Therefore the number of sunny lines satisfies\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{b\\in D}(n+1-b). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   To make (1) as easy as possible we choose the rows with the smallest sizes, i.e. the rows with the largest indices.  \n   Hence we may assume\n   \\[\n   D=\\{n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   For these rows\n   \\[\n   n+1-b = 1,2,\\dots ,k,\n   \\]\n   and the right‑hand side of (1) becomes\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k} i = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently any feasible configuration must satisfy\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k=0\\) inequality (2) holds trivially.  \n   For \\(k=1\\) we have \\(\\frac{1\\cdot2}{2}=1\\), so (2) is also satisfied.  \n   If \\(k\\ge2\\) then \\(\\f