unny line exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Key observation on non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three families  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=\\text{const}\\}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently a non‑sunny line meets each row, each column and each anti‑diagonal in **at most one** point.\n\n4. **Counting rows that are not covered horizontally.**  \n   Let \\(h\\) be the number of horizontal lines among the \\(n\\) given lines.  \n   The rows that are *not* covered by a horizontal line form a set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R=\\{b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid \\text{no line }y=b\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is \\(r=n-h\\).  \n   For any \\(b\\in\\mathcal R\\) the whole row \\(R_b\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-h\\) non‑horizontal lines.  \n   Because each non‑horizontal line can contribute at most one point to a fixed row, we obtain the **row inequality**\n\n   \\[\n   |R_b|=n+1-b\\;\\le\\;n-h\\qquad\\text{for every }b\\in\\mathcal R. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (1) forces all rows whose length exceeds \\(n-h\\) to belong to the horizontal family.  \n   Since the lengths of the rows are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\), condition (1) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   h\\;\\ge\\;n-(n-h)=n-(n-h)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;h\\ge n-k. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (Recall that at most \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; therefore \\(h\\le n-k\\).  Combining the two bounds yields \\(h=n-k\\).)  \n   Hence **all** non‑sunny lines are horizontal; the remaining \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Rows that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \n   Because every non‑sunny line is horizontal, the only rows not covered horizontally are the last \\(k\\) rows  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R=\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total number of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each sunny line meets any given row in at most one point, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\) points of the rows in \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \n   Therefore we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k+1\\le 2k\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k\\ge1. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (4) is satisfied for every \\(k\\ge1\\); however, we still have to respect the fact that **each point of a row must be covered by a distinct sunny line** because a single sunny line cannot hit two different points of the same row.  \n   Consequently the number of points that have to be covered by sunny lines (the left‑hand side of (3)) cannot exceed the number of sunny lines \\(k\\).  Hence we obtain the sharper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k(k+1)\\le 2k\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k\\le1. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (For \\(k=0\\) the inequality is trivial; for \\(k\\ge1\\) we may divide by \\(k\\).)\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (5) shows that **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist**.  \n   The constructions of Step 2 exhibit configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
