-r,\\qquad y=n-c,\\qquad z=n-d,\n   \\]\n\n   so that \\(x,y,z\\ge 0\\) and \\(x+y+z\\ge 2n+2\\).  \n   The right‑hand side of (3) becomes \\(xy-z\\).  \n   Because \\(x+y+z\\ge 2n+2\\) and each of \\(x,y\\) is at most \\(n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) rows and \\(n\\) columns), the product \\(xy\\) is minimized when \\(x=y=n\\) and \\(z\\ge 2\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   xy-z\\ge n\\cdot n- (n-2)=2. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **at least two points of \\(S_n\\) are uncovered by all non‑sunny lines**.\n\n   Let \\(P_1,P_2\\) be two such uncovered points.  \n   By definition each of them lies on a missing row, a missing column and a missing diagonal.  \n   Consequently the line \\(P_1P_2\\) is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); in any case it is *not* sunny.  \n   Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2\\).  \n\n   Since we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines, covering the two uncovered points requires at least two distinct sunny lines.  \n   If \\(k=2\\) the two sunny lines would have to be exactly those covering \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \n   However, the third point of \\(S_n\\) that lies on the same missing row as \\(P_1\\) (or the same missing column as \\(P_2\\)) is also uncovered, because a missing row (or column) contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   That third point cannot be covered by either of the two sunny lines (they already contain \\(P_1\\) or \\(P_2\\) and a sunny line cannot pass through a second point of the same missing row/column/diagonal).  \n   Hence a third sunny line would be necessary, contradicting \\(k=2\\).\n\n   The same contradiction appears for any \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   Therefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of § 2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.  \n   The impossibility argument of § 4 shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For all }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
