ion for \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   From (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\min\\{x,y,z\\}\\le\\frac{x+y+z}{3}= \\frac{2n+k}{3}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4) and (5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|\\le k\\frac{2n+k}{3}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, Lemma 1 gives the lower bound (2).  \n   Recall that \\(t=n+k-z\\) and that, by (1), \\(z\\le n\\); hence \\(t\\ge k\\).  \n   Substituting the smallest possible value \\(t=k\\) into (2) gives the *weakest* lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|\\ge\\frac{k(k-1)}2+k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (6) and (7) we obtain the necessary inequality for the existence of a configuration:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\;\\le\\;k\\frac{2n+k}{3}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (8) simplifies to  \n\n   \\[\n   3k(k+1)\\le 2k(2n+k)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k+3\\le 4n .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 2\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right‑hand side of (9) is at least \\(12\\), while the left‑hand side is at most \\(k+3\\le k+3\\).  \n   The only way (9) can hold with \\(k\\ge 2\\) is when \\(k\\le 1\\), a contradiction.  \n   Hence **no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) show that these two values are attainable.  \n   The impossibility argument above shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For all }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]"
