\n* \\(r\\) = number of rows among the non‑sunny lines,\n* \\(c\\) = number of columns among the non‑sunny lines,\n* \\(d\\) = number of diagonals among the non‑sunny lines.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nr+c+d=n-k. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nDefine the *missing* sets  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{b\\mid R_b\\text{ is **not** used}\\},\\qquad\nY=\\{a\\mid C_a\\text{ is **not** used}\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nZ=\\{t\\mid D_t\\text{ is **not** used}\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\nx=|X|=n-r,\\qquad y=|Y|=n-c,\\qquad z=|Z|=n-d .\n\\]\n\nFrom (3.1) we obtain the fundamental relation  \n\n\\[\nx+y+z=2n+k. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Points that must be covered by sunny lines  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **not** covered by the non‑sunny lines iff its row, column and diagonal are all missing; i.e.\n\n\\[\nb\\in X,\\;a\\in Y,\\;a+b\\in Z .\n\\]\n\nDenote the set of such points by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\in Y,\\;b\\in X,\\;a+b\\in Z\\}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(U\\) have to be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  A lower bound for \\(|U|\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt:=x+y-n\\;(=n+k-z)\\ge k . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality follows from \\(z\\le n\\).)\n\nConsider first the pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in Y,\\;b\\in X\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); ignore the diagonal condition.  \nAmong all choices of the sets \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) with given sizes \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), the number of such pairs is **minimal** when the missing rows and columns are the largest possible ones, namely  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{n-x+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad Y=\\{n-y+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn this extremal situation the admissible pairs are exactly the integer solutions of  \n\n\\[\nu+v\\le t-1,\\qquad u,v\\ge 0,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(u=a-(n-y+1)\\) and \\(v=b-(n-x+1)\\).  \nThe number of solutions is  \n\n\\[\nP=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nNow we re‑impose the diagonal