\((i-1,1),(1,i-1),(j-1,1),(1,j-1)\\) lies on\n      exactly one of the two new lines;\n* (iii) the two new lines are distinct from each other and from any\n      diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(S_{i,j}^{(1)}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{j-1-1}{1-(i-1)}=\\frac{j-2}{2-i}= -\\frac{j-2}{i-2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is finite, non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\) because \\(j\\neq i\\).\nHence \\(S_{i,j}^{(1)}\\) is sunny; the same computation works for\n\\(S_{i,j}^{(2)}\\).  \nBoth lines contain the two points indicated in their definition, and the\nfour points are pairwise distinct because \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nSince a sunny line cannot be parallel to any diagonal, the new lines are\ndifferent from all \\(D_s\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Increasing the number of sunny lines by one).\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers\n\\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nIf a diagonal line \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}\\), then the\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}'=\\bigl(\\mathcal{L}\\setminus\\{D_s\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_s\\},\n\\qquad\nS_s:\\;(s-1,1)\\text{\u2013}(1,s-2),\n\\]\n\nalso consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and has one\nmore sunny line than \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(S_s\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{s-2-1}{1-(s-1)}=-\\frac{s-1}{s-2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S_s\\) is sunny.\nThe point \\((s-1,1)\\) was covered only by \\(D_s\\); after the replacement it\nlies on \\(S_s\\).  The second point \\((1,s-2)\\) lies on the diagonal\n\\(D_{s-1}\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal{L}'\\); consequently every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) remains covered.  Because the slope of \\(S_s\\) is\ndifferent from the slopes of all remaining lines, \\(S_s\\) is distinct from\nthem. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 (even part).*  \nWrite \\(k=2m+r\\) where \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and \\(0\\le 2m\\le n\\).  \nChoose \\(m\\) disjoint unordered pairs\n\\(\\{i_1,j_1\\},\\dots ,\\{i_m,j_m\\}\\) from the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (possible because \\(2m\\le n\\)).  \nStarting from the baseline family \\(\\{D_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\), replace for\neach pair \\(\\{i_\\ell ,j_\\ell\\}\\) the two diagonals \\(D_{i_\\ell}\\) and\n\\(D_{j_\\ell}\\) by the two sunny lines given in Lemma&nbsp;2.\nAfter all replacements we have a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines containing exactly \\(2m\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Step 2 (odd part, if needed).*  \nIf \\(r=1\\) (i.e. \\(k\\) is odd), then \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) still contains at least\none diagonal line \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\) (because \\(n\\ge 3\\) and we have\nremoved at most \\(2m\\le n-1\\) diagonals).  \nApply Lemma&nbsp;3 to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and this diagonal \\(D_s\\); the\nresulting family \\(\\mathcal{F}'\\) has exactly \\(2m+1=k\\) sunny lines and still\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a\nconfiguration satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality.\n\nThe construction shows that **every** integer in the interval\n\\([0,n]\\) is attainable.  Since we are required to use exactly \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); obviously it\ncannot be negative.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, the answer is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}.\n\\]"
