te coverage).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Definition of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ and its cardinality  \n\n> \u201cLet $\\mathcal{P}_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}$.  \n> The cardinality of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ is $|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nFor each integer $s$ with $2\\le s\\le n+1$, the equation $a+b=s$ with $a,b\\ge1$ has exactly $s-1$ solutions. Summing gives $\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=n(n+1)/2$. The computation is correct.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Trivial upper bound  \n\n> \u201cWe use exactly $n$ distinct lines, therefore at most $n$ of them can be sunny: $0\\le k\\le n$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Direct consequence of the definition of $k$ (the number of sunny lines among $n$ lines).  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Case $k=0$  \n\n> \u201cFor each integer $s$ with $2\\le s\\le n+1$ consider the line $D_{s}: x+y=s$. \u2026 Each $D_{s}$ is parallel to the line $x+y=0$; consequently none of them is sunny. \u2026 Thus a configuration with exactly $k=0$ sunny lines exists.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Every point $(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ satisfies $a+b=s$ for a unique $s$, so it lies on $D_{s}$.  \n- Each $D_{s}$ has slope $-1$, i.e. is parallel to $x+y=0$, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n- There are $n$ distinct values of $s$ (from $2$ to $n+1$), giving $n$ distinct lines.  \n\nAll requirements are satisfied.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Case $k=1$  \n\n> \u201cTake the $n-1$ vertical lines $V_{i}: x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)$ and the line $L: y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)+1$. \u2026 The slope of $L$ equals $-\\dfrac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1$; hence $L$ is sunny. \u2026 Every point of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ with first coordinate $\\le n-1$ lies on some $V_{i}$; the only point not covered by the verticals is $(n,1)$, and $(n,1)\\in L$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Each $V_{i}$ is vertical (non\u2011sunny) and covers all points with $a=i$.  \n- For $a=n$, the only admissible $b$ is $b=1$, giving the point $(n,1)$. Substituting $x=n$ into $L$ yields $y=1$, so $(n,1)$ lies on $L$.  \n- $L$ has slope $-1/(n-1)$, which is not $0$, $\\infty$, or $-1$ for $n\\ge3$.  \n\nThus we have $n-1$ non\u2011sunny lines and one sunny line, covering all points.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Impossibility of $k=2$\n\n##### 5.1 Claim about the number of points covered by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n> \u201cA vertical line $x=c$ can contain at most $n$ points of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ \u2026 A horizontal line $y=c$ can contain at most $n$ points. \u2026 A line of slope $-1$ \u2026 contains exactly $s-1\\le n$ points.  \n> Hence the $n-2$ non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most $(n-2)n$ points.  \n> Because $|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2> (n-2)n$ for every $n\\ge3$, at least one of the two sunny lines must contain **three** points of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$.\u201d\n\n**Verification of the inequality:**  \nCompute the difference:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-2)n=\\frac{n^2+n-2n^2+4n}{2}=\\frac{-n^2+5n}{2}= \\frac{n(5-n)}2.\n\\]\n- For $n=3,4$ the difference is positive, so the inequality holds.  \n- For $n=5$ the difference is $0$ (equality).  \n- For $n\\ge6$ the difference is negative, i.e. $\\frac{n(n+1)}2<(n-2)n$.\n\nThus the statement \u201c$|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|> (n-2)n$ for every $n\\ge3$\u201d is **false** for $n\\ge5$.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (factual mistake).\n\n##### 5.2 Claim that a sunny line can contain at most two points  \n\n> \u201cIf such a line contained three distinct points $(a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})$ of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$, then the three points would have either the same sum $a+b$ (forcing slope $-1$) or the same difference $a-b$ (forcing slope $0$ or $\\infty$). \u2026 consequently a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Three collinear points with the same **difference** $a-b$ lie on a line of slope $1$, which is sunny (slope $1\\neq0,\\infty,-1$).  \n- Example for $n\\ge6$: points $(1,2)$, $(2,3)$, $(3,4)$ all belong to $\\mathcal{P}_{n}$ and lie on the line $y=x+1$ (slope $1$). Hence a sunny line can contain three (indeed many) points.  \n\nTherefore the assertion \u201ca sunny line can contain at most two points\u201d is **incorrect**.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n##### 5.3 Consequence for $k=2$  \n\nBecause the two preceding statements are false, the derived conclusion \u201ca configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any $n\\ge3$\u201d is **unjustified**. In fact, for $n=5$ a construction (e.g. the one given in the analysis) shows that $k=2$ is possible.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (the conclusion rests on false premises).\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Construction for every $k\\ge3$\n\n##### 6.1 Claim that all $\\ell_i$ are sunny  \n\n> \u201cBecause $k\\ge3$, this slope is never $0$, never $\\infty$ and never $-1$; consequently every $\\ell_{i}$ is sunny.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nFor $i=1$, the slope $(i-1)/(k-1)=0$, so $\\ell_{1}$ is the horizontal line $y=1$, which is **parallel to the $x$\u2011axis** and therefore **not sunny**.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (the claim is false).\n\n##### 6.2 Claim that the family $\\{\\ell_i\\}$ covers all points of $\\mathcal{Q}_{k}$  \n\n> \u201cTake any point $(x,y)\\in\\mathcal{Q}_{k}$. \u2026 Set $i:=y$. Substituting this value of $i$ into (6) gives $x$ back, showing that $(x,y)$ satisfies the equation of $\\ell_{i}$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nEquation (6) is $x=1+\\dfrac{(y-1)(k-1)}{i-1}$. Substituting $i=y$ yields\n\\[\nx=1+\\frac{(y-1)(k-1)}{y-1}=1+(k-1)=k,\n\\]\nso the only point of $\\mathcal{Q}_{k}$ that lies on $\\ell_{y}$ is $(k,y)$. In general $(x,y)$ with $x\\neq k$ does **not** satisfy $\\ell_{y}$. Hence the argument that every point of $\\mathcal{Q}_{k}$ lies on some $\\ell_i$ is false.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (incorrect algebraic reasoning; the covering claim fails).\n\n##### 6.3 Translation back to the original coordinates  \n\n> \u201cUndo the translation \u2026 define $L_{i}: y=\\frac{i-1}{k-1}\\bigl(x-(t+1)\\bigr)+1$ \u2026 Each $L_{i}$ is sunny \u2026 the family $\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}$ covers exactly the points of $\\mathcal{T}_{k}$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nSince the underlying family $\\{\\ell_i\\}$ does **not** cover $\\mathcal{Q}_{k}$, the translated family $\\{L_i\\}$ cannot cover $\\mathcal{T}_{k}$. Moreover, $L_{1}$ inherits the non\u2011sunny horizontal slope from $\\ell_{1}$, contradicting the requirement that **exactly** $k$ lines be sunny.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (the construction does not meet the problem\u2019s conditions).\n\n##### 6.4 Overall conclusion for $k\\ge3$  \n\n> \u201cThus a configuration exists for every integer $k$ with $3\\le k\\le n$.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The preceding construction is invalid, so the existence claim is unsupported. No alternative construction is provided.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (the conclusion rests on a flawed construction).\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Final conclusion  \n\nThe solution correctly handles the cases $k=0$ and $k=1$, but it **fails** to justify the impossibility of $k=2$ (the inequality and the \u201cat most two points per sunny line\u201d claim are false) and the proposed construction for $k\\ge3$ (contains non\u2011sunny lines, does not cover all required points, and therefore does not yield exactly $k$ sunny lines). Consequently the answer \u201c$\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}$\u201d is **incorrect**. The solution contains multiple Critical Errors that invalidate the entire argument."
